FAMILY_DEPTH = {
    "deformation-family": {
        "human_problem": "The human problem is that the original picture is often too tangled to reason about directly, but an uncontrolled simplification can answer the wrong question. The family asks how to change the picture while keeping the exact data the problem cares about: endpoints, boundaries, gluing, forbidden crossings, side choices, or the count being protected.",
        "first_principles": "Start by naming the legal moves and the fixed data. Then move the object through those moves until it is easier to inspect. If endpoints, boundaries, gluing rules, forbidden crossings, side behavior, or a protected count survive every step, the simplified picture speaks for the original picture and not merely for itself.",
        "how_it_works": "A deformation proof has four parts: the starting object, the contract of allowed motion, the protected feature, and the simpler endpoint. The proof fails if any part is left unnamed or unchecked. The endpoint is convincing only because the route to it was legal and carried the protected feature all the way.",
        "course_examples": "The disk-connection puzzle, surface classification by surgery, intersection invariance, and the treatment of vector-field defects all use this family. In each case, motion is not a side effect; it is the proof method. The examples differ in object, but they share the same demand: the easy final picture must be reached through legal steps.",
        "failure_mode": "The common failure is to simplify by a move that the problem does not permit. Cutting a loop, sliding an endpoint, passing through an obstacle, or changing a boundary condition may make the picture easier while solving the wrong problem. A deformation argument is broken exactly when the shortcut changes the question it was supposed to preserve.",
    },
    "counting-family": {
        "human_problem": "The human problem is that a drawing can change while the underlying obstruction remains. A count gives the reader something stable enough to compare before and after the change, but only if it was designed for the changes the problem permits. The family asks which part of the changing picture deserves memory.",
        "first_principles": "Do not count everything that is visible. First list the legal local changes: subdividing a cell, creating two crossings, sliding a defect, reversing a side choice, or passing through a controlled special moment. Then count the feature whose changes cancel under those moves. Sometimes the count is alternating, as in Euler characteristic. Sometimes it is signed, as in intersection number or index. Sometimes parity is enough because only even changes can occur.",
        "how_it_works": "A surviving-count proof identifies which local changes can happen, then shows those changes leave the chosen total alone. After that local audit, one honest computation in an easy picture constrains every legally related picture and can forbid a desired outcome or force at least one event. The local check is what lets one computation speak for many pictures.",
        "course_examples": "Euler characteristic counts cells with alternating signs. Intersection number counts meetings with plus and minus signs. Poincare-Hopf counts vector-field defects and ties their total to the surface. These are different objects but the same proof instinct: design the arithmetic so fake changes cancel and the real obstruction remains.",
        "failure_mode": "The common failure is to use a count that changes under harmless redrawings. A raw crossing count, for example, can rise or fall when a canceling pair is created, so it is not the protected quantity. The reader must ask what happens under one legal local change before trusting the total.",
    },
    "surface-family": {
        "human_problem": "The human problem is that surfaces look simple nearby but can behave differently as wholes. This family asks how patches, boundaries, holes, handles, gluing rules, and orientation combine into whole-surface facts that local inspection alone misses completely. It teaches why a small ordinary-looking neighborhood is not the whole story.",
        "first_principles": "Cut the surface into manageable pieces, understand what happens on each piece, and then glue the bookkeeping back together. The global result is not guessed from one patch; it is forced by how all patches fit, how edges match, and whether local choices can survive a full trip around the surface.",
        "how_it_works": "Surface bookkeeping uses decompositions, orientation choices, boundary terms, and cancellation. It is careful about what is local and what is global, because many surface obstructions appear only after a full trip around the object or a full sum over its pieces. The proof asks what the completed surface permits all local accounts to do together.",
        "course_examples": "The Mobius strip reveals global one-sidedness. Surface classification tracks handles and crosscaps. Euler characteristic records whole-surface type. Poincare-Hopf shows that vector fields must obey the surface's total count. The same surface facts keep reappearing in different language, from paper-strip travel to the final question of whether arrows can agree everywhere.",
        "failure_mode": "The common failure is to reason locally and assume the whole surface behaves the same way. A Mobius strip defeats that assumption immediately, and later orientation-dependent counts fail without global consistency across the surface. Another failure is counting pieces before reading the gluing rule that tells what surface those pieces actually make.",
    },
    "embedding-family": {
        "human_problem": "The human problem is to know whether one object can sit inside another without forbidden collisions. A drawing may suggest an answer, but this family asks for a reason that covers every drawing and every legal attempt. It replaces drawing frustration with an audit of the object, the room, and the forbidden moves.",
        "first_principles": "Separate the object's required connections from the room supplied by the surrounding surface or space. If every legal placement would force a forbidden meeting, the obstruction belongs to the topology, not to a bad drawing. The object and the receiving room must both be named before the placement question is meaningful.",
        "how_it_works": "Embedding arguments track crossings, over-under information, winding, linking, endpoint order, and surface room. They ask whether deformation can remove intersections or whether a count proves that some relation survives every allowed placement in the space under study. The proof must explain why the obstruction defeats all legal placements, not just one sketch.",
        "course_examples": "Path puzzles in a disk, planar graph questions, knots and links, and intersection theory all belong here. The family teaches the reader to distinguish accidental crossings from forced ones by checking what legal motion can remove. Later, the same habit helps separate a visible meeting from a signed intersection that cannot be dismissed.",
        "failure_mode": "The common failure is to confuse a failed attempt with a proof of impossibility. A topological obstruction must show that every legal attempt fails, not only the first drawing tried or the first plausible arrangement. A second failure is forgetting data that the flat drawing hides, such as over-under order or the surface that supplies the routes.",
    },
    "motion-family": {
        "human_problem": "The human problem is to understand motion when exact equations or trajectories are unavailable. This family asks what the shape of possible states can force before anything is solved second by second or computed point by point. It lets the course reason about behavior by first making the space of possible behavior honest.",
        "first_principles": "Represent each complete possible state as a point in a new space. Complete means enough information to recover the situation the question cares about: positions, angles, boundary conditions, contacts, and forbidden collisions. Motion becomes a path or rule on that space. Fixed points, equilibria, blocked routes, and forced passages become questions about the shape of the allowed state space.",
        "how_it_works": "The family translates physical or dynamical behavior into shape: graph of a rule against self-agreement, vector-field defects, index sums, or paths in a configuration space. Once translated, earlier tools such as deformation and surviving counts apply, but the translation remains part of the proof. The model must say what one point means and what one path means.",
        "course_examples": "The center-of-gravity demonstration, Brouwer fixed point theorem, vector fields, equilibria, Poincare-Hopf, and late applications all use this family. The course ends here because it shows topology acting on behavior, not only on static pictures. A rule, a mechanism, or an arrow field becomes something the earlier shape tools can inspect.",
        "failure_mode": "The common failure is to model the state space carelessly. If the states, boundaries, forbidden positions, or allowed motions are wrong, the topological conclusion may no longer describe the physical system being studied at all. The page must therefore treat modeling as part of the proof, not as a preface before the real argument.",
    },
}


