FAMILY_ESSAYS = {
    "deformation-family": [
        "Deformation arguments are the course's way of replacing a hard object by an easier one without losing the question. The first step is not to move anything. The first step is to state the contract: what may bend, what must stay attached, which crossings are forbidden, which endpoints stay fixed, and which boundaries still count as boundaries. Only after that contract is clear does motion become a proof method.",
        "Many topological questions cannot be settled from the first drawing. A tangled drawing may hide a simple answer; a simple-looking drawing may hide an obstruction. Deformation gives the reader permission to simplify, but only under rules that preserve the feature being tested. The endpoint of a deformation proof is not evidence by itself. The evidence is the whole legal journey from the original picture to the easier one.",
        "From first principles, deformation separates appearance from obligation. Appearance is the visible wiggle, bend, angle, or placement. Obligation is the part the problem refuses to let go: boundary order, no crossing, one-sidedness, a signed total, or the meaning of a map. A deformation proof moves the appearance while guarding the obligation. If the obligation survives, the easier picture can answer the harder one.",
        "This family connects the early disk-path puzzle to later vector-field cleanup. In the disk puzzle, the protected data include endpoint order and the no-crossing rule. In surface surgery, the protected data include the surface being studied and the effect of each cut or gluing step. In vector-field cleanup, the protected data include the total index unless a controlled event explains how local defects change. The proof is not saying that all drawings are equally good. It is saying that the allowed drawings form a connected range of possibilities, and the protected fact stays the same throughout that range. Ask for the route, not only the before-and-after pictures.",
        "If every allowed step preserves the relevant fact, then the final easy picture can speak for the original hard picture. One illegal step can make the final picture irrelevant. Deformation works because its freedom is disciplined.",
    ],
    "counting-family": [
        "Surviving-count arguments begin with a practical problem: the visible picture changes too much. Crossings can appear, cells can be subdivided, arrows can be redrawn, and defects can move. A raw count of everything usually fails because it records accidental clutter. The course therefore asks for a count designed to ignore the harmless changes while keeping the obstruction that matters.",
        "Euler characteristic does this by alternating pieces. Intersection number does this by using signs. Vector-field index does this by recording how arrows turn around a defect. Parity does it more coarsely by keeping only evenness or oddness. These examples look different on the surface, but they share one idea: count the part of the situation that legal motion cannot erase.",
        "From first principles, the count is engineered around the allowed local changes. If a face is split, the added edge and face must cancel in the Euler account. If two intersections are born together, their signs must add to zero. If a vector-field defect splits into pieces, the local indices must keep the same total. The count is not chosen because it looks important; it is chosen because the harmless changes leave it alone.",
        "A good count can prove impossibility or forced existence without listing every possible drawing. Once the count is known in one honest version of the object, every legally related version has to obey it. If the total is odd, at least one event must remain. If the signed total is nonzero, complete cancellation is impossible. If the Euler account is fixed, any decomposition of the same surface must pay the same final bill. A small number can carry a large conclusion when it represents all legal redrawings, not one snapshot.",
        "Test every surviving-count argument by asking what one allowed change does to the total. If the proof cannot explain that local event, the count has not earned trust. If it can, the arithmetic becomes a way to hold the whole moving picture still.",
    ],
    "surface-family": [
        "Surface bookkeeping is needed because a surface can lie to local inspection. Every tiny patch of a sphere, torus, disk, or Mobius strip may look like an ordinary piece of paper. The difference appears when patches are glued into a whole: a route may return reversed, a boundary may add a term, a handle may create a new loop, or a missing disk may change what can be filled.",
        "This family teaches the reader to track the whole surface without losing the local pieces. Divide the surface into manageable parts, count or orient those parts, then check what survives when they are put back together. Euler characteristic, boundary orientation, surface classification, and Poincare-Hopf all depend on this discipline.",
        "From first principles, a surface is both local room and global instruction. Local room lets the course draw curves, arrows, neighborhoods, and small crossings. Global instruction says how those local rooms attach to each other. A sphere, torus, and Mobius band can all look locally ordinary while placing different demands on loops, side choices, and vector fields.",
        "The family connects early strip examples to late dynamics. The Mobius strip shows a local side choice failing after a full trip. Euler characteristic shows a surface count surviving many decompositions. Poincare-Hopf shows the same surface count controlling the total defect of an arrow field. The object has changed, but the bookkeeping habit is the same: local facts must be reconciled with the whole surface.",
        "Local freedom is not the same as global freedom. A patch may allow an arrow, a side choice, or a sign, while the completed surface refuses to let all those local choices agree at once. Surface bookkeeping is how the course finds the refusal.",
    ],
    "embedding-family": [
        "Drawing and embedding arguments ask whether there is enough room for required connections. A failed drawing is not enough, because a better drawing may exist. The family therefore separates the object that must be placed from the space that must receive it. Which endpoints are fixed? Which crossings are forbidden? Can one strand pass through another? Does the surrounding surface have holes or handles that provide alternate routes?",
        "This is where knots, links, planar graphs, path puzzles, and intersection arguments meet. Each problem asks whether a relation can be removed by legal motion or whether it belongs to the shape of the situation. A crossing on the page may be accidental. A linking relation or forced intersection may survive every attempt to redraw.",
        "From first principles, embedding is a room test. The object has demands: these points must connect, this loop must close, these strands may not pass through each other. The surrounding space has allowances: routes around holes, extra dimensions, boundaries, or a flat page with no overpass. The proof asks whether the allowances can satisfy the demands without violating the rules.",
        "This family also teaches the difference between a drawing and an obstruction. A drawing is one attempt. An obstruction is a reason every legal attempt fails. That reason may be boundary order in a disk, linking in space, a signed intersection count, or a shortage of dimensions. The course keeps returning to this distinction because visual failure alone is too weak.",
        "The method is not testing drawing skill. It is proving that all legal drawings face the same constraint. The surface or surrounding space supplies only certain routes, and sometimes those routes are not enough. When that happens, the failure belongs to the topology of the placement problem.",
    ],
    "motion-family": [
        "Motion-through-state arguments are the course's bridge from shapes to behavior. Instead of following a mechanism directly, the method lists its possible states and treats each state as a point in a new space. A motion is then a path through that space. A rule becomes a map of the space to itself. A forbidden physical arrangement becomes a wall, hole, or missing region in the state space.",
        "This family explains why fixed points, vector fields, configuration spaces, and physical examples belong with surface topology. They are all ways of asking what behavior the shape of possibility permits. The course does not need every equation of motion to say something meaningful. If the state space has the right obstruction, some positions, coincidences, defects, or blocked motions are forced before detailed solving begins.",
        "From first principles, the method replaces time with possibility. Instead of asking where the system goes at every instant, ask what one complete state is, what states are allowed, and what paths through the state space would represent legal motion. A fixed point becomes self-agreement of a rule on that space. An equilibrium becomes a zero of an arrow field. A blocked motion becomes a missing path. A forbidden collision becomes a removed point, wall, or region that can split the space of possible motions.",
        "The same tools now speak about behavior because behavior has been turned into a shape of possibilities. Deformation tracks legal motion through possible states. Invariants record what cannot change along such motion. Fixed-point theorems and index sums force special states. The late applications are not separate from the earlier paper-strip lessons; they use the same object, move, protected fact, and consequence pattern.",
        "The detail that keeps the method honest is modeling. The state space must contain the right freedoms and remove the right forbidden states. One point must not hide two physically different situations unless the problem truly treats them as the same. A path must mean a legal continuous motion, not merely a line drawn in a convenient diagram. If that translation is wrong, the topological conclusion may be true about the model but false about the physical situation. Modeling is therefore part of the proof, not a story attached before the proof begins.",
    ],
}


